WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.485
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.250
SANYAL: Welcome to the
opening lecture of the series

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.440
Myths About America.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.030
This lecture series
has been organized

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.730
by MIT's Special Program for
Urban and Regional Studies--

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.720
in short, SPURS.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.630
SPURS is institutionally located
in the Department of Urban

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.805
Studies and Planning at MIT.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.970
I'm Bish Sanyal,
director of SPURS,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.360
and it is an honor for me
to welcome Professor Howard

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.080
Zinn, the speaker this evening.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.420
I also want to welcome all of
you, the fellows, the students,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.410
faculty, and staff.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.440
Perhaps there are others
in the audience who

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.260
do not fit any of
these categories,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.230
but are eager nonetheless
to participate

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.790
in our deliberations
on myths about America.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.410
A welcome to you all.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.380
Let me provide a very
brief description

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:31.440
of SPURS, the program which is
hosting this lecture series.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.900
For some of you
who may not know,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.810
SPURS was created in 1967, a
critical historical moment,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.750
much like the time
we are living now.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.840
And it just created for
MIT career professionals

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.850
from around the world who
wanted to spend a year at MIT

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.120
reflecting on their
work experience

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.230
in a setting very different
from their work environment,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.800
and one which provided
opportunities for reflection

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.960
and learning.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.120
Now, a majority of the
fellows in the program

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.900
are supported by the
Fulbright Exchange Program

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.260
for practitioners, named
after the late senator, Hubert

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.120
Humphrey.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.910
One key objective
of this program

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.590
is to cultivate international
understanding of the US

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:30.550
by other nations in Asia,
Africa, Latin America,

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.400
and the Middle East.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.300
As you well know, the need
for such an understanding

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.500
is more important
now than ever before.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.460
The selection of the team and
the speakers for this year's

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.870
lecture series was made with
this particular objective

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.670
in mind.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.120
We assumed that most
international fellows

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.880
and students come to the US with
certain preconceived opinions

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.440
and perceptions regarding
American society, politics,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.330
and economy.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.700
The goal of this
lecture series is

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:14.250
to subject such perceptions
to critical scrutiny,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.280
drawing on historical evidence.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.330
We hope that such scrutiny
of both negative and positive

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:25.590
myths about America, as
held by others, and also

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:29.340
by Americans themselves,
will help the fellows better

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.130
understand this nation.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.805
A few individuals
and institutions

00:03:34.805 --> 00:03:37.860
deserve thanks for the
organization of this lecture

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.320
series.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.110
First, the Department of Urban
Studies and Planning at MIT,

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.110
which is currently Professor
Larry Vale, who I see

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.850
is in the audience.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:57.390
[INAUDIBLE] and [INAUDIBLE],,
without whose help we could not

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.430
have organized this
lecture series.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.750
And finally, I want to
thank the fellows themselves

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.140
for their intellectual
curiosity and eagerness

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.380
to better understand the US.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.280
We are honored that this process
of critical understanding

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.630
is to be started with a lecture
by Professor Howard Zinn, who

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:22.660
does not need much
of an introduction.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.990
A People's History
of the United States,

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.650
which Professor Zinn
published in 1980,

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.690
is a book that has inspired many
who could, for the first time,

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.540
see their own contributions
to the nation's

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.700
historical evolution thanks to
this new history from below.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.740
But Howard Zinn is not a
person whose experience

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.910
is confined to
non-traditional social groups

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.270
at the bottom of
American society.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:59.160
He's a decorated war veteran who
flew bombing missions in Europe

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.030
during World War II,
returned from the war

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:07.260
and attended New York
University on the GI Bill,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.310
and then went on to attend
Columbia University, where

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:16.680
he earned a master's degree
and PhD in political science.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.340
Later, he became the chair
of history and social science

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.140
at Spelman College in Atlanta,
a college for black women,

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.910
where he became deeply involved
in civil rights movements.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.580
Howard Zinn's
understanding of the US

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.890
is based as much on such
concrete firsthand experience

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.640
as on academic studies
of historical events.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.670
The combination of these
two sources of knowledge

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:49.270
has cultivated a mind
which is at once sharply

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.560
analytical, deeply
empathetic, and patriotic too,

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:57.530
in a way in which is
necessary for our times.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.460
So with much admiration,
I present to you

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.310
the opening speaker of our
lecture series, Professor

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.520
Howard Zinn, will
speak this evening

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.870
on the topic of the myth
of American exceptionalism.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.070
Professor Zinn will
speak for an hour--

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.090
or 45 minutes to an hour--

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:20.350
at the end of which he will have
an exchange of views with you.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:21.265
Thank you.

00:06:21.265 --> 00:06:23.245
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:40.075 --> 00:06:43.790
ZINN: Thank you,
Professor Sanyal,

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.207
for that nice introduction.

00:06:46.207 --> 00:06:47.040
Can you all hear me?

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.600
And also for inviting me.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:57.830
Not everybody invites me.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.910
And sometimes I have
to go places uninvited.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.295
But it's good too good
to be here at MIT.

00:07:11.234 --> 00:07:14.870
Are any of you back there
having trouble hearing me?

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.550
No?

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.060
One person?

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:21.850
Okay.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.760
American exceptionalism--
the myth of American

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.160
exceptionalism--

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:45.300
and by the way, when I
told a friend of mine

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:51.270
that I was going to give a talk
on American exceptionalism,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:57.048
she immediately got
in touch with somebody

00:07:57.048 --> 00:07:58.590
who had been a
student of hers, and I

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.700
think who is now
teaching, and who

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.000
sent me a paper on
American exceptionalism.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.480
But I will not read
her paper, although she

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:15.680
has some interesting good
and useful things to say.

00:08:20.065 --> 00:08:22.830
And one of the
points she makes is

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:24.400
that, by American
exceptionalism,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.180
we don't mean that the United
States is simply unique, simply

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:28.680
different.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:29.850
All countries are unique.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.580
All countries are different.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.240
Exceptionalism suggests
more than that.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.539
It suggests superiority.

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:44.880
It suggests something that
all of us living in the United

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.990
States have encountered
a lot, and that

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:53.880
is self-congratulation.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.530
We are fond in the United States
of congratulating ourselves

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:03.410
for how wonderful we are
and how we are the best,

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.160
we are the greatest,
we are the strongest,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.600
we are the most prosperous,
we are the freest,

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.150
we are the most democratic.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:20.580
And yes, we are number one.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.730
And we are, in fact,
number one in many things.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.560
And we are very good--

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:27.752
really, very good--
in many things.

00:09:27.752 --> 00:09:29.460
And those are the
things I probably don't

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:33.340
have to tell you about, because
anybody who is in the United

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:37.200
States, even for a
short time, knows,

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.093
because the things that the
United States is good about

00:09:41.093 --> 00:09:42.135
are immediately apparent.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.980
But that's not enough.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:51.950
That doesn't tell
the whole story.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.970
And if we only
dwell on what we all

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.160
acknowledge are some of
the remarkable things

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:58.930
about the United
States, we will be

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:01.840
missing something very
important-- something, in fact,

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:09.550
so important that we'll be
shocked if something happens

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:20.620
one day that arouses
us from our complacency

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:27.370
and that makes us wonder how it
is that a country so gifted, so

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:32.920
special, so superior,
should experience suddenly

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:36.460
a disaster that
nobody can explain.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:42.160
And yes, when 9/11
happened, that

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:46.410
was an occasion perhaps for
reflection on ourselves--

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:51.250
a sober and critical
reflection on ourselves.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:55.370
That did not happen in the
higher reaches of government.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.520
The people at the
top of the government

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.950
are not given to self-reflection
or to self-criticism.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:04.440
No.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:11.030
Instead, the reaction was a
kind of hysterical reaction,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:16.170
a reaching out--

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:20.820
well, reaching is a euphemism

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:27.180
War, violence,
attack, do something--

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:31.890
but no, no reflection.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.790
So I think that that
kind of examination

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:39.640
of who we are as a
country is needed,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.880
so we won't be unguarded,
so we won't be shocked when

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.550
something terrible happens--

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.950
so we will perhaps
begin to understand.

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:58.700
This idea of self-congratulation
sometimes manifests itself this

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:05.390
way, that if you criticize
the United States government--

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:09.980
a lot of people
here have probably

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:14.180
experienced this, as
anybody who has ever

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:17.610
uttered a word of criticism--

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:22.430
you're met with the exhortation.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:29.590
Well, why don't you
go somewhere else?

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.800
Well, sometimes, they
say, why don't you

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:35.940
go back to where you came
from, which might be Brooklyn.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:40.950
But it's somehow
go somewhere else.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:46.510
A friend of mine
who's a comedian--

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.030
I have friends of mine
who are comedians,

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.890
but who don't know it--

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:54.550
but this is a guy who is a
comedian and who knows it,

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:56.635
but he's also serious.

00:12:56.635 --> 00:12:58.010
Some of you maybe
have maybe even

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.808
encountered him, because
he used to do comedy here

00:13:00.808 --> 00:13:01.600
in the Boston area.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.870
His name is Barry Crimmins.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:08.890
And in his serious comedy,
he would very often

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.800
be critical of certain
things that the United

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.473
States had done in the world.

00:13:13.473 --> 00:13:14.890
And people would
say to him, well,

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.760
why don't you move
somewhere else?

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:19.010
And he would say, I don't
want to move somewhere else,

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:20.470
because I don't want
to become the victim

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:21.745
of American foreign policy.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:32.230
Well, this notion of
superiority and exceptionalism

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:33.040
starts early.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.380
And [? Sarah ?] [? Murton, ?]
who wrote this book,

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:42.100
points out that it
starts as early as--

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:45.430
well, here in
Massachusetts Bay Colony,

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:48.670
in 1630, the colony
had just begun

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:54.940
and the Governor Winthrop
utters those words, which

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.820
centuries later,
will be repeated

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.760
by Ronald Reagan, who was a
great student, as you know,

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:02.680
of early American history.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:11.200
And the Winthrop talked about
the Massachusetts Bay Colony

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.760
as a city on a hill.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.330
I think Reagan
embellished a little

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.290
and talked about a
golden city on a hill.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.330
Well, the idea of
a city on a hill--

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.580
you probably heard that
expression a number of times--

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:29.170
the idea of a city on
a hill is a nice one,

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:33.360
because it suggests a model.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:36.910
It suggests setting an example.

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.390
Actually, it suggests
what, in fact,

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:44.380
George Bush has spoken
of, when he said,

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:47.860
we are a beacon of
liberty and democracy.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:53.230
And if that was what we were, if
that's all we are, if that's--

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.840
we're a city on a hill
that people can look to,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:58.420
and the people can learn
from, and people can admire,

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:04.840
and people can emulate, that
is a wonderful thing to be.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:11.830
But it doesn't stop there with
just being a city on a hill.

00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:17.650
In fact, you can see it, because
just a few years after Governor

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:21.640
Winthrop utters these words
about being a city on a hill--

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:28.330
just a few years later, the
people in the city on a hill

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:35.560
move out to massacre
the Pequot Indians, who

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:41.290
seemed to think they
belong on this land.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:48.010
And there's a
description of that,

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:55.270
which was William Bradford,
one of the early settlers

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:59.350
in Massachusetts at that time,
a contemporary of Winthrop,

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.590
wrote a history of the
Plymouth Plantation.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:06.490
And he talks about
how Captain Mason,

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:14.840
attacking a Pequot village,
said, "We must burn them."

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:20.200
And then Bradford
reports, "Those

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.990
that escaped the fire were
slain with a sword, some hewn

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:28.210
to pieces, some run
through with their rapiers,

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:31.210
as they were quickly dispatched,
and very few escaped.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:34.360
It was conceived that this
thus destroyed about 400

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:35.970
at this time.

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:38.920
It was a fearful sight to
see them frying in the fire,

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:41.980
and the streams of blood
quenching the same.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:44.380
But the victory seemed
a sweet sacrifice,

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:47.230
and they gave prayers
thereof to God,

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:50.050
who had wrought so
wonderfully for them

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.290
and given them so
speedy a victory over so

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.720
proud and insulting an enemy."

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:02.830
And very early on,
there's an association

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:09.520
between what the government
does and what God approves of.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:18.170
And that process of not being
justice city on the hill,

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:26.290
but of moving out, of
expanding, continued.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:29.920
That's a persistent
fact of American history

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.770
going all the way back
to those first settlers,

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:35.920
and coming down to
the present day--

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:42.700
the persistence of expansion
into somebody else's territory,

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.510
and occupying that territory,
and dealing harshly

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:51.690
with the people who
resist that occupation.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.425
And one of the
things that, when you

00:17:54.425 --> 00:17:57.050
study the American Revolution in
school, one of the things they

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.210
don't tell you--

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:02.840
although we know about the
good things of the American

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.580
Revolution-- independence
from England,

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:07.950
and the Declaration of
Independence, and all of that--

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.460
but generally, they
skip over the fact

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:16.970
that independence from
England for the colonists

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:20.090
was disastrous for the Indians.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.020
Because it meant
that, from now on,

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:25.250
the colonists, who had been
restrained by the British

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:28.580
in their Proclamation
of 1763, setting

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:32.270
a line, a boundary within which
the settlers were supposed

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:35.100
to stay so as not to encroach
into Indian territory,

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.600
this boundary line
was now erased.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:42.560
And now, the colonists-- now,
with the revolution victorious,

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.600
with independence from
England, the colonists

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:48.710
could move westward
into Indian territory.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:52.010
And of course, that
process continued

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.850
on and on with the
kind of description

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:59.690
that you heard of the Pequot
massacre occurring again

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:05.180
and again in American
history, as we moved across

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:10.350
to the west coast and down
to the Gulf of Mexico.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.220
And this notion of
God being involved,

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:16.970
this notion of being
divinely ordained,

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.920
that notion continued.

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:23.120
In the middle of
the 19th century,

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.680
on the eve of the
War with Mexico,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.268
when the United States
had just annexed Texas--

00:19:29.268 --> 00:19:31.310
a lot of Americans don't
know that Texas was just

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:33.470
part of Mexico.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:34.970
A lot of people in
the United States

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:38.060
don't know that California
was once part of Mexico.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.520
They wonder, why do they
have all these Spanish names

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:41.490
out there?

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:44.030
San this, and Santa this, and--

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:46.470
yeah, well.

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:50.270
But on the eve of
war with Mexico,

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:55.950
this famous phrase was
coined by a writer and editor

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.250
named John O'Sullivan.

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:01.225
He spoke about manifest destiny.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:09.920
And he said, "It was
our manifest destiny

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:14.570
to overspread the continent
allotted by providence

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:18.380
for the free development of our
yearly multiplying millions."

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:23.360
Providence, God, continued to
be involved in an expansion.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:30.950
When the United States
went into the Philippines

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:36.590
at the beginning of the 20th
century, President McKinley--

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:39.500
some of you may know
this, because it's--

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.190
not everything that I say
to people is an obscure fact

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:46.580
and sometimes I tell people
that they really know.

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:49.430
Maybe often, I tell people
things that they really know.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.350
Sometimes I think
everything I tell

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:56.390
people is something they really
know, but I say it anyway.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:21:00.380
But you may have heard that
McKinley said that the decision

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:08.000
to invade the Philippines
came one night when he prayed,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:13.010
and God told him to
take the Philippines.

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:21.800
And so this is a very
dangerous kind of idea.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:25.615
Once you have God's permission
to do what you want,

00:21:25.615 --> 00:21:32.510
you need no criterion
of human morality.

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:39.470
And with President Bush--

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:42.380
if you don't mind me
skipping immediately

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:48.470
a lot of American history
to President Bush--

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:50.300
and it's well
known that Bush has

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:55.370
a special relationship to
God and that Bush invokes God

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:56.870
every chance he gets.

00:21:56.870 --> 00:21:58.540
He's not the only president.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:21:59.800
I mustn't be too hard.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:01.550
He's not the only
president who does this.

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:04.380
The invoking God is
a very common thing

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:07.700
all through American
history, but Bush

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:11.060
has made a specialty of it.

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:19.030
Now, I'm going to tell
you something where

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:22.570
I don't know if this is true.

00:22:22.570 --> 00:22:26.670
Not all historians will admit
that the things they tell you

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:36.370
may not be true, but
it sounds true to me.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:42.020
How's that for scientific
bit of evidence?

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:45.790
And that is an article appeared
in the Israeli newspaper

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:48.910
Haaretz.

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:51.130
Any of you remember
an article that

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:54.370
appeared in the
Israeli newspaper

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:58.000
Haaretz, in which
the reporter talks

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.710
about talking to Palestinian
leaders who had met with Bush?

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:07.780
Okay, finally I'm telling
you something that is not

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.650
only fairly new, but dubious.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:14.652
But according to
this reporter, he

00:23:14.652 --> 00:23:17.110
spoke to his Palestinian leader,
and the Palestinian leader

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:20.110
reported on his
conversation with Bush.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:24.310
And as he reported,
Bush told him,

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:29.800
"God told me to strike at
al-Qaeda, and I struck them.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:34.660
And then he instructed me to
strike at Saddam, which I did.

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:37.760
And now, I am determined to
solve the problem in the Middle

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:38.260
East."

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:41.515
Well, as I say, who knows?

00:23:45.117 --> 00:23:47.200
It's one of the situations
where you get something

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:48.310
second and third-hand.

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:53.470
It's just that it's
plausible, knowing Bush.

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.310
Whether it's true or
not, it's plausible.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:00.430
It fits everything else.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:06.940
Now, I'll give you a piece of
evidence, which is I think,

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:09.250
more credible.

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:12.080
That is that it's
closer to home.

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:17.740
This is the president of the
Ethics and Religious Commission

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:22.210
of the Southern Baptist
Convention, a friend of Bush,

00:24:22.210 --> 00:24:24.310
and says that Bush told
him during the election

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:31.000
campaign, quote, "I believe
God wants me to be president."

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.630
Now, I would consider
that interference

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:37.560
in a free election.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:47.080
And God wants me
to be president.

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:50.090
And this actually was
reported in the newspaper.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:56.450
And this man,
Richard [? Lamb, ?]

00:24:56.450 --> 00:25:01.430
who says that Bush told him this
during the election campaign,

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:04.790
he resented the fact
that it was quoted,

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:07.100
"I believe God wants
me to be president,"

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:09.470
but it was quoted
out of context.

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:14.030
Context was that Bush
followed this up by saying,

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:16.782
but if that doesn't
happen, it's Okay.

00:25:16.782 --> 00:25:23.590
Well, I thought
that's reasonable.

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:32.840
He's willing to change his mind,
if God changes his or her mind.

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:35.420
Okay.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:43.810
This idea of God giving strength
to whatever the government does

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:46.480
is something that is
true not just with Bush,

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:52.290
but at the higher reaches
of American government,

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:59.360
the people around the
bush, and Justice Scalia.

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:07.150
Justice Scalia, I don't know
how closely you watch him, but--

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:13.690
this is an aside because I just
got this in the mail today,

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:18.610
and that was something-- a
publication of the Dramatists

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:20.920
Guild, in which they haven't--

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:24.070
they reproduce a talk
that the playwright Tony

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:29.570
Kushner gave at Bard College,
and it's all about Scalia.

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:32.770
But that's just an aside.

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:41.240
Scalia seriously believes
that God is the source

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.250
of governmental power.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:45.170
And he has said this
again and again.

00:26:51.955 --> 00:26:57.210
He spoke in-- well,
two years ago,

00:26:57.210 --> 00:27:00.450
at the University of Chicago
Divinity School, said,

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.045
"Government is the
minister of God.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:12.660
Government derives its
moral authority from God."

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:21.390
And this is a dangerous
idea in anybody's hands.

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:24.000
Scalia seems to be
overlooking the fact

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.860
that the Declaration
of Independence

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:30.280
suggests that
government gets its--

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:32.378
gets authority-- excuse me--

00:27:32.378 --> 00:27:33.045
from the people.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.270
Government gets its
authority from the people.

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:41.710
It's answerable to the people.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.940
If the government
doesn't make sure

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.290
that people have an equal
right to life, liberty,

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:49.080
and the pursuit of
happiness, it is then

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:51.363
the people who
have the right to,

00:27:51.363 --> 00:27:52.780
as the Declaration
of Independence

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:57.460
says, to alter or
abolish the government.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:02.262
But I suppose Scalia's a
strict constitutionalist,

00:28:02.262 --> 00:28:03.720
and the Declaration
of Independence

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:05.250
is not part of the Constitution.

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:06.917
In fact, the Declaration
of Independence

00:28:06.917 --> 00:28:09.210
is not a legal document at all.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:14.040
It's just the most
idealistic statement

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.860
to come out of the American
Revolution, and one

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.680
to be put aside as soon
as you win the revolution.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:32.700
So this idea of God being a
source of governmental power,

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.460
it's always dangerous
in anybody's hands.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:40.860
It's dangerous when people call
themselves the chosen people.

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:42.180
It's dangerous there too.

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:46.875
But if these people don't
have a lot of power,

00:28:46.875 --> 00:28:48.500
there's not much they
can do with that.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:53.667
They can enjoy the fact that
they are the chosen people.

00:28:53.667 --> 00:28:55.250
If there's some point
in history where

00:28:55.250 --> 00:29:02.420
they develop the power to do
something to somebody else,

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:04.580
well, then it becomes serious.

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:11.090
And we've seen that there are
people who in Israel who have--

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:20.420
who justify the occupation
of the West Bank

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:25.610
on very pragmatic grounds of
security and self-defense.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.950
But there are others--

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:34.220
there are religious fanatics
who justify the occupation

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.220
on the basis of yes,
being the chosen people,

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:42.280
and being chosen
by God to do this.

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:48.470
Of course, there are important
people, important Jews

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:53.510
who have opposed this idea of
calling themselves the chosen

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:58.400
people, considering what
terrible things have

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:04.400
been done in the name of
being selected by God.

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:06.800
After all, the
Nazi storm troopers

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:13.150
had on their belts
Gott mit uns--

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:16.650
God with us.

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:19.040
So it's a dangerous
concept when--

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.140
especially when the
people who believe

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:25.130
they are structured by God
have great military power.

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:27.760
And the Nazis, with
their military power.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:31.460
And with that kind
of divine ordination,

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:35.990
we know what they did in Europe.

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:39.080
And now, you have
the United States,

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:43.700
and you have a
government which assures

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:49.940
us gets its power from God.

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:52.400
And in the hands of
the United States,

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:55.670
this is a dangerous doctrine,
because [INAUDIBLE] simply--

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:59.510
because of the great
power of the United States

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:03.170
to do whatever they
think is God's will, that

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:09.800
is a nation with 10,000
nuclear weapons, a nation

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:13.940
with military bases in
100 different countries

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:17.840
with its warships on every sea.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:24.740
When you couple that power with
the notion of divine sanction,

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:30.890
then the world is in danger.

00:31:30.890 --> 00:31:50.770
And this idea that the United
States should use its power

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:53.680
in the world, should
revel in its being number

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:57.520
one, that [INAUDIBLE]
became especially important

00:31:57.520 --> 00:31:58.990
that at the end of World War II.

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:00.448
Because at the end
of World War II,

00:32:00.448 --> 00:32:04.330
the United States did become the
preeminent power in the world.

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:08.110
Yes, there was a
Soviet Union, but it

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:11.530
was the United States
that was the greatest

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:14.560
economic and [INAUDIBLE]
atomic weapons,

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:19.160
the greatest military
power in the world.

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:23.200
And at the end of
World War II Henry

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:28.990
Luce, who is of the I
guess most important

00:32:28.990 --> 00:32:31.030
nongovernmental
people, one of most

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:34.480
powerful nongovernmental
people in the country--

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:41.110
the owner of a vast chain of
media enterprises, the man

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:47.020
who owned Time,
Life, and Fortune--

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:50.770
not just the magazines,
but the things themselves.

00:32:50.770 --> 00:33:02.080
But Henry Luce said that this
would be the American Century.

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:07.780
As he put it, the victory now,
at the end of World War II,

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:12.640
gave the United States the
right to exert upon the world

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:16.810
the full impact of our
influence for such purposes

00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:22.735
as we see fit, and by
such means as we see fit.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:30.960
And that idea, that
we do what we want,

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:35.220
that this is the American
Century, that in fact,

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:39.480
persisted, and was acted
out through the rest

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:44.020
of the 20th century, because
the United States continued

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:44.770
to expand.

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:49.710
Even with the Soviet Union
there and soon acquiring

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:54.000
its own nuclear weapons,
the United States

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:55.920
continued to expand into--

00:33:55.920 --> 00:34:00.120
its influence into the oil
regions of the Middle East

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:06.480
by this special arrangement
with Saudi Arabia,

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:20.480
into Asia with Japan, and into--
certainly into Latin America.

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:26.760
And there were some setbacks--
the setback in Vietnam.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:30.960
But the Philippines remained
a very important base

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.790
for the United States.

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:34.290
And yes, and soon,
the United States

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:36.570
had military bases
all over the world,

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:40.409
and soldiers and sailors
stationed all over the world.

00:34:40.409 --> 00:34:51.250
And the Cold War
with the Soviet Union

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:56.239
did not really do much
to stop this expansion.

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:59.920
In fact, the Cold War, the
existence of the Soviet Union

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:02.290
as a rival, the
existence of something

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:06.070
called a communist
threat in the world

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:12.460
gave the United
States a justification

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:15.940
for doing so much of what
it was doing in the world--

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:18.250
for expanding its
power in the world,

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.030
for overthrowing this
government or that government,

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.930
or moving troops here
and moving troops there,

00:35:23.930 --> 00:35:29.080
and all justified
by the necessity

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:33.730
of stopping communism.

00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:40.380
Now, you can see that--

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:50.030
I think-- historically,
that this was

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:52.220
an artificial justification.

00:35:52.220 --> 00:35:55.980
And by that, I mean
that this wasn't

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:58.587
the real reason for American
expansion in the world.

00:35:58.587 --> 00:36:00.170
The real reason for
American expansion

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:03.860
was not to stop communism.

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:08.300
After all, that expansion was a
continuation of expansion which

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:10.830
had gone on for a long time.

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:13.760
If you want to see the
limits of that idea

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:16.620
that it was the
Soviet Union and Cold

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:20.110
War which was the reason for
America United States expanding

00:36:20.110 --> 00:36:21.860
its power in the world,
all you have to do

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:27.620
is ask the question, what
was going on before 1917?

00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:30.952
What was going on before there
was a Bolshevik Revolution?

00:36:30.952 --> 00:36:32.660
At that point, the
United States was just

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:35.650
being minding its own business?

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:36.150
No.

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:41.960
Not long before there's
a Bolshevik Revolution,

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:44.720
the United States was engaged
in expanding its power

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:47.195
into the Caribbean,
into the Pacific.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:58.640
So what happened, of course,
is that the Cold War ended.

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:02.210
That justification ended.

00:37:02.210 --> 00:37:07.580
But very soon after,
9/11 happened.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:15.485
And once 9/11 took place,
another justification appeared.

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:24.170
Terrorism replaced communism
as a kind of simple answer

00:37:24.170 --> 00:37:27.050
to the question of,
why are we going here?

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:28.020
Why are we going there?

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:29.480
Why are we making war here?

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.700
Why are we sending troops there?

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:35.582
And in the '50s, it was
why are we overthrowing

00:37:35.582 --> 00:37:37.790
the government of Guatemala,
the government of Chile,

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:38.930
or the government of Iran?

00:37:38.930 --> 00:37:41.030
Well, it's communism.

00:37:41.030 --> 00:37:44.730
And now, it's terrorism.

00:37:44.730 --> 00:37:46.700
And it has a certain
plausibility,

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:51.080
because there was a
reality to communism,

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:55.080
but what we did in relation that
reality went far beyond what

00:37:55.080 --> 00:37:56.030
the real threat was.

00:37:56.030 --> 00:37:58.370
There's a reality to
terrorism, but what

00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:04.790
we do in relation to
terrorism does not quite

00:38:04.790 --> 00:38:11.090
meet the requirement
of having an effect--

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:14.070
serious effect on terrorism.

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:18.530
And it does suggest
that there are

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:26.060
motives deeper than the
idea of fighting terrorism

00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:29.690
for the American
move into the Middle

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:33.810
East in these past few years.

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:45.300
So one year after
9/11, President Bush

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:50.930
announces the national
security strategy.

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:53.130
And this basically
lays out the principles

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:57.240
for American foreign
policy, and lays out

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:13.650
the principles of unilateralism,
and the preemption,

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:19.440
and making sure
that no power can

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:25.290
rival the power of the
United States in the world.

00:39:25.290 --> 00:39:30.100
And essentially,
this is a declaration

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:33.040
that the United States
could ignore the UN Charter,

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:37.330
because if you accept
pre-emption, then

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:40.210
you are violating the
principle of the UN Charter

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:47.750
that you're only supposed
to make war in self-defense.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:52.300
And I suggest you're also
violating the principles laid

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:57.070
down in the Nuremberg trials,
for which then Nazi leaders

00:39:57.070 --> 00:39:59.680
were put on trial and hanged.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:07.960
And that is for aggressive war,
for preemptive war, [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:07.960 --> 00:40:11.890
war not in self-defense.

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:20.060
So this is a very serious thing,
this declaration of principles

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:23.710
and national security strategy.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:36.620
And we shouldn't think it was
just a Bush Republican idea.

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:44.190
Very often, we like to think
that before Bush, things

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:52.980
were okay and that Bush
represents a dramatic departure

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:55.320
from whatever we had before.

00:40:55.320 --> 00:41:00.360
But in fact, preemption
is something that--

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:03.440
after all, the United
States has made war before,

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:05.220
had invaded countries before--

00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:07.120
well, as we did
in Southeast Asia,

00:41:07.120 --> 00:41:11.250
which was certainly not a
matter of defending ourselves.

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:15.940
And the United States has done--

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:18.480
has made war unilaterally--

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:20.430
and this was before Bush--

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:24.360
made war unilaterally or
carried out bombing missions

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:25.320
unilaterally.

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:33.560
Sometimes creating a kind of
cloak of internationalism,

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:36.230
by bringing NATO or
something or by bringing

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:40.250
in the UN, as in
Korea, but basically

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:44.450
an American enterprise.

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:50.450
And in fact, under Clinton,
Madeleine Albright said at one

00:41:50.450 --> 00:41:54.060
point-- some of you may remember
this-- she said, we will--

00:41:54.060 --> 00:42:02.240
if possible, we will act in
the world multilaterally.

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:05.750
But if necessary, we
will act unilaterally.

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:19.370
And Clinton was not averse
to acting unilaterally

00:42:19.370 --> 00:42:20.930
and preemptively.

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:32.300
And what happens in the
present situation is that--

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:36.260
I must say this,
that very often,

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:38.510
liberals in the United States--

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:39.890
people who are not for Bush--

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:50.150
yes, liberals in the
United States accept--

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:56.250
with qualifications here and
there-- but accept basically

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:59.840
the Bush principles.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:05.220
And the reason
for this, I think,

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:11.640
is that 9/11 had a very
powerful psychological effect

00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:17.410
on everybody in America.

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:20.760
But I think there are many
people in the United States--

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:25.560
liberal intellectuals
who are really, I think,

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:35.400
so affected emotionally by what
happened on 9/11 that I think

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:38.310
it began to distort
their thinking--

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:40.490
I know they wouldn't
like me saying this--

00:43:40.490 --> 00:43:44.270
distort their thinking about
what was happening in the world

00:43:44.270 --> 00:43:46.920
and about America's
role in the world.

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:53.440
And they were sort of becoming
hysterical over terrorism.

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.740
And again, yes, there's
a reality of terrorism.

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:58.790
There's a difference between
dealing with terrorism

00:43:58.790 --> 00:44:03.230
in a intelligence and real way
and becoming hysterical over

00:44:03.230 --> 00:44:05.570
terrorism in the way
that actually liberals

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:07.430
in the 1950s--

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:11.870
those of you who know some
of the history of the United

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:14.720
States in the 1950s during
the Cold War period,

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:17.420
during what is called the
McCarthy period, you might

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:24.290
remember that there were
not just McCarthy people,

00:44:24.290 --> 00:44:26.270
but liberal people--

00:44:26.270 --> 00:44:31.580
Democrats-- who
reacted hysterically

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:35.000
to what they considered
the communist threat.

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:36.540
To the point where
Hubert Humphrey,

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:40.910
who was sort of a quintessential
American political liberal--

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:48.590
Hubert Humphrey suggested the
notion of internment camps

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:53.090
for dangerous people
in the United States

00:44:53.090 --> 00:44:56.720
in times of crisis.

00:44:56.720 --> 00:44:59.450
And I think we're
seeing something

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:01.240
of the same phenomenon.

00:45:01.240 --> 00:45:04.700
Because I was reading
in the liberal magazine,

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:06.840
The American Prospect,
where the editors,

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:09.470
in their recent issue, they--

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:15.140
their premise is that the
greatest immediate threat

00:45:15.140 --> 00:45:18.230
to our lives and liberties
are Islamic terrorists

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:19.160
with global reach.

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:25.250
And therefore, when facing
a substantial, immediate,

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:27.950
and provable threat,
the United States

00:45:27.950 --> 00:45:30.080
has both the right
and the obligation

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:33.380
to strike preemptively,
and if need be,

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:37.820
unilaterally against terrorists
or states that support them.

00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:44.420
Preemptively, and if need
be, unilaterally-- well,

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:47.030
that is the Bush doctrine.

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:50.960
And of course, they
qualify this by saying,

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:54.050
facing a substantial,
immediate, and provable threat.

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:58.910
The problem is that
those who decide

00:45:58.910 --> 00:46:04.160
whether a threat is,
in fact, substantial,

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:06.560
immediate, provable--

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:10.190
people who decide that will not
be the liberal intellectuals

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:12.260
who formulated
this, but the people

00:46:12.260 --> 00:46:14.300
who run the government
of the United States.

00:46:14.300 --> 00:46:17.240
They will decide it,
just as Bush decided it

00:46:17.240 --> 00:46:19.867
when he decided to go
to war in Afghanistan,

00:46:19.867 --> 00:46:21.575
and when he decided
to go to war in Iraq.

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:31.920
These ideas of unilateralism,
and preemption, and--

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:36.180
these are not new.

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:40.940
The whole history of the
United States in the world

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:45.080
is a history of
expansion based on these,

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:49.333
and rationales given like
the rationales given today

00:46:49.333 --> 00:46:50.750
when we go to war,
about spreading

00:46:50.750 --> 00:46:55.260
liberty, and democracy,
and civilization.

00:46:55.260 --> 00:47:01.950
And we declared war on
Mexico in 1846, again,

00:47:01.950 --> 00:47:06.050
for the purpose of teaching
civilization to the Mexicans.

00:47:06.050 --> 00:47:10.970
And then we went
into Cuba in 1898

00:47:10.970 --> 00:47:14.780
to bring liberty to the Cubans.

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:18.060
In fact, there's always--

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:20.120
or a lot of times--
a certain measure

00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:22.100
of truth to these statements.

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:27.500
As we did expel Spanish
domination from Cuba,

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:32.990
we liberated the Cubans from
Spain, but not from ourselves.

00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:36.200
Because once Spain was gone,
the United States moved in,

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:39.470
corporations moved in, the
American military moved in,

00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:45.290
Americans wrote-- rewrote
the Cuban constitution.

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:49.790
But their rationale was we are
bringing freedom to the Cubans.

00:47:49.790 --> 00:47:51.830
And then, of course,
going into the Philippines

00:47:51.830 --> 00:47:54.380
and bringing civilization.

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:58.450
As McKinley said, "We will
Christianize and civilize

00:47:58.450 --> 00:47:59.510
the Filipinos."

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:08.360
Because we are
different, we are better.

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:12.140
At the time of the invasion
of the Philippines,

00:48:12.140 --> 00:48:15.020
the American Secretary
of War, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:48:15.020 --> 00:48:23.330
said a very classical statement
of American exceptionalism.

00:48:23.330 --> 00:48:26.480
He said, "The American
soldier's different

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:29.630
from all other soldiers
of all other countries

00:48:29.630 --> 00:48:33.110
since the world began.

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:36.380
He is the advanced guard
of liberty and justice,

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:41.120
of law and order, and
of peace and happiness."

00:48:41.120 --> 00:48:43.710
American soldier is different.

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.620
Well, of course,
now, right now--

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:49.760
immediately now, in
the wake of Abu Ghraib,

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:53.060
in the wake of all these
revelations coming out every

00:48:53.060 --> 00:48:59.975
day about torture, and
atrocities, and so on, that's--

00:49:02.740 --> 00:49:04.700
doesn't sound right.

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:06.640
But you might say, well,
[INAUDIBLE] could not

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:08.415
anticipate Abu Ghraib.

00:49:08.415 --> 00:49:09.790
But he didn't have
to anticipate,

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:11.440
because at the
time, he was saying

00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:15.010
this the United States was
already committing atrocities

00:49:15.010 --> 00:49:21.850
and massacres in the Philippines
by these American soldiers, who

00:49:21.850 --> 00:49:25.750
were different from
soldiers all over the world.

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:38.900
The history of American
expansion in the world

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:46.370
is not a history which is
taught very much in our schools,

00:49:46.370 --> 00:49:49.340
or even in our colleges
and universities.

00:49:54.790 --> 00:49:56.860
We have something called
diplomatic history.

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:00.700
That's a discipline.

00:50:00.700 --> 00:50:02.560
Discipline is
diplomatic history,

00:50:02.560 --> 00:50:04.720
and that's what our
history is very often.

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:07.000
It's diplomatic history,
and we diplomatically

00:50:07.000 --> 00:50:13.720
treat the American foreign
policy in the world.

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:20.270
Because if young
people in our schools

00:50:20.270 --> 00:50:26.570
learned the history of United
States expansion in the world,

00:50:26.570 --> 00:50:33.350
if they learned the history
of the massacres and invasions

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:39.370
that accompanied American
expansion in the world,

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:42.080
they could not possibly believe
the President of the United

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:45.050
States when he gets
up before the nation

00:50:45.050 --> 00:50:49.430
and says we're going
into this country to--

00:50:49.430 --> 00:50:51.990
for liberty and democracy.

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:56.600
This is Operation Enduring
Freedom, and so on.

00:50:56.600 --> 00:50:58.160
But that history is not there.

00:50:58.160 --> 00:51:01.880
And this misuse of
history is-- it continues

00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:06.080
to be perpetuated by
our political leaders,

00:51:06.080 --> 00:51:10.160
and not really
caught or criticized

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:14.210
by that part of the American
culture which is supposed

00:51:14.210 --> 00:51:17.720
to check up on and criticize
what the government does--

00:51:17.720 --> 00:51:21.480
that is, the press, the media.

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:25.550
And so you have Bush
appearing, as he

00:51:25.550 --> 00:51:27.980
did a couple of years ago,
before the Philippine National

00:51:27.980 --> 00:51:31.130
Assembly and saying to
the Philippine National

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:33.620
Assembly, the--

00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:38.800
I don't know.

00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:42.880
He said, "America is proud of
its part in the great story

00:51:42.880 --> 00:51:44.650
of the Filipino people.

00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:47.560
Together, our soldiers
liberated the Philippines

00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:50.238
from colonial rule."

00:51:50.238 --> 00:51:52.030
Well, the people in
the Philippine National

00:51:52.030 --> 00:51:53.140
Assembly sitting there--

00:51:56.800 --> 00:52:00.820
you wonder, must have taken
a lot of self-restraint

00:52:00.820 --> 00:52:05.300
to just sit there and
listen to this-- liberated

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:07.160
the Philippines.

00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:12.410
600,000 or so Filipinos
died in this long war

00:52:12.410 --> 00:52:15.050
against the Filipinos.

00:52:15.050 --> 00:52:18.320
And at the end of it, when the
United States was triumphant,

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:20.510
it did not bring liberty
to the Philippines.

00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:22.910
It brought decades and
decades and decades

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:25.640
of military dictatorship
for the Philippines.

00:52:32.950 --> 00:52:36.520
I remember there was a point,
I don't know, about a year ago,

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:42.150
where the Mexican
ambassador to the UN

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:45.840
said something undiplomatic
about the United States

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:47.670
and Mexico.

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:54.630
He said something about how the
United States has been treating

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:58.480
Mexico as its backyard.

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:03.130
He was immediately
reprimanded by Colin Powell,

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:04.290
who said that he--

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:07.470
this man did not
understand the history

00:53:07.470 --> 00:53:13.040
of US-Mexican relations.

00:53:13.040 --> 00:53:18.030
And in fact, he was soon
removed from his post.

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:20.040
That's how much clout we have.

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:25.783
He was then removed from his
post as Mexican ambassador

00:53:25.783 --> 00:53:26.700
to the United Nations.

00:53:35.140 --> 00:53:37.830
So yes, without
that history, you

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:43.020
might actually
believe Bush when he

00:53:43.020 --> 00:53:49.170
says, in his recent inaugural
address, that it is--

00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:50.080
how did he put it--

00:53:50.080 --> 00:53:58.950
[INAUDIBLE] mission
of the United States

00:53:58.950 --> 00:54:01.890
to spread liberty
around the world.

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:03.960
Spreading liberty around
the world, as he put it,

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:05.580
is the calling of our time.

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:11.710
And if you read the newspapers,
including calling the so-called

00:54:11.710 --> 00:54:12.550
liberal press--

00:54:12.550 --> 00:54:14.500
The New York Times,
The Washington Post--

00:54:14.500 --> 00:54:16.780
right after the--

00:54:16.780 --> 00:54:22.570
Bush's inaugural address,
you saw a flurry of praise

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:27.610
for what Bush had
said as if people were

00:54:27.610 --> 00:54:30.550
in the editorial rooms
of these newspapers

00:54:30.550 --> 00:54:35.080
were so eager to hear those
words about spreading liberty

00:54:35.080 --> 00:54:38.110
in the world, and as
if everything else that

00:54:38.110 --> 00:54:42.550
has been reported from Iraq
over the past two years

00:54:42.550 --> 00:54:48.880
is meaningless in light
of these beautiful words

00:54:48.880 --> 00:54:52.460
uttered by George Bush.

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:54.380
But all I would
have to do would be

00:54:54.380 --> 00:54:58.130
to just, with a
very short memory,

00:54:58.130 --> 00:55:03.080
remember that a couple
of days before Bush's

00:55:03.080 --> 00:55:08.060
inaugural address--

00:55:08.060 --> 00:55:10.930
a couple of days
before that, there

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:16.480
was a photo in the New York
Times, which some of you

00:55:16.480 --> 00:55:17.240
may have seen.

00:55:17.240 --> 00:55:25.030
It showed an Iraqi girl
crouching, bleeding,

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:27.340
and according to
the caption, she

00:55:27.340 --> 00:55:32.530
was screaming because
her parents had just

00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:39.760
been shot to death by
Americans firing on their car.

00:55:39.760 --> 00:55:43.060
And of course,
[INAUDIBLE] well, we--

00:55:43.060 --> 00:55:46.340
claim the military--
we'll investigate.

00:55:46.340 --> 00:55:47.980
There were warning shots.

00:55:47.980 --> 00:55:52.530
How do you distinguish a
warning shot from a shot?

00:55:52.530 --> 00:56:00.670
But in any case, to me, this was
some remarkable juxtaposition

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:02.320
of--

00:56:02.320 --> 00:56:07.030
but also a testament to the loss
of memory, even a memory that

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:09.700
can last a couple
of days, to see

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:20.240
this eager acceptance of
Bush's words about liberty

00:56:20.240 --> 00:56:29.453
and this idea of
American exceptionalism.

00:56:34.090 --> 00:56:37.390
You were told I would
speak for an hour or so.

00:56:37.390 --> 00:56:40.763
I'm taking advantage
of every minute of it.

00:56:40.763 --> 00:56:42.430
Because when I heard
it, I said, no, I'm

00:56:42.430 --> 00:56:44.603
not going to speak for an hour.

00:56:44.603 --> 00:56:46.270
Actually, I want to
speak for two hours.

00:56:53.480 --> 00:56:57.770
What this idea of special
American dispensation

00:56:57.770 --> 00:56:58.970
in the world--

00:56:58.970 --> 00:57:03.680
what it leads to
is an abrogation

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:10.460
of all sorts of responsibilities
to the human race,

00:57:10.460 --> 00:57:12.530
to everybody else in the world.

00:57:12.530 --> 00:57:14.570
And it means that
the United States

00:57:14.570 --> 00:57:17.990
is exempt from these
responsibilities.

00:57:21.140 --> 00:57:23.600
I told you about
Madeleine Albright

00:57:23.600 --> 00:57:28.820
declaring that we have a right,
if necessary, to be unilateral.

00:57:28.820 --> 00:57:35.660
When she said that,
Henry Kissinger said,

00:57:35.660 --> 00:57:40.440
this principal of our right
to take unilateral action,

00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:45.970
he said, should not
be universalized.

00:57:45.970 --> 00:57:48.775
That's an interesting thought.

00:57:51.870 --> 00:57:55.540
It's a good principle for us.

00:57:55.540 --> 00:57:58.870
But no, it shouldn't
be universalized.

00:57:58.870 --> 00:58:01.360
No.

00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:04.960
There's a long list of instances
in which the United States has

00:58:04.960 --> 00:58:09.490
declared itself exempt from
international agreements,

00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:12.760
and international laws,
and the UN Charter,

00:58:12.760 --> 00:58:17.620
and the Convention on
Biological Weapons--

00:58:17.620 --> 00:58:20.950
which was actually
signed some years ago,

00:58:20.950 --> 00:58:24.040
but which didn't have any
teeth, didn't have any force.

00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:29.170
And when it was proposed
to enforce this Convention

00:58:29.170 --> 00:58:30.940
on Biological Weapons
a few years ago,

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:34.030
the Bush administration
said no way.

00:58:34.030 --> 00:58:35.920
It wouldn't go along with that.

00:58:35.920 --> 00:58:38.920
And I think most of us know
the United States has not

00:58:38.920 --> 00:58:42.070
gone along with the 100
or more nations that

00:58:42.070 --> 00:58:45.220
have outlawed landmines.

00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:49.390
And just the other
day, I spoke to a--

00:58:49.390 --> 00:58:51.610
listened to actually--
and spoke with--

00:58:51.610 --> 00:58:54.420
presentation by a surgeon--

00:58:54.420 --> 00:58:58.030
an Italian surgeon
who, for 15 years,

00:58:58.030 --> 00:59:04.970
has been doing surgery on war
victims in every possible war

00:59:04.970 --> 00:59:06.790
zone of the world.

00:59:06.790 --> 00:59:10.120
Most of the people
he operates on,

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:12.520
which includes a
lot of amputations,

00:59:12.520 --> 00:59:17.470
are children, and much of
that comes from landmines,

00:59:17.470 --> 00:59:21.130
100 million of which are
strewn around the world.

00:59:21.130 --> 00:59:24.040
But the United States wants
to be exempt from the notion

00:59:24.040 --> 00:59:26.200
that we should outlaw landmines.

00:59:26.200 --> 00:59:30.070
And of course, we continue
[INAUDIBLE] things

00:59:30.070 --> 00:59:36.190
outlawed by the Geneva
Convention, and cluster bombs,

00:59:36.190 --> 00:59:36.880
and napalm.

00:59:49.790 --> 00:59:51.620
So what's the answer?

00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:57.680
What's the answer to this
very dangerous notion

00:59:57.680 --> 01:00:02.840
of American exceptionalism,
of our right

01:00:02.840 --> 01:00:06.920
to do as we will in the world?

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:14.200
Well, I guess the answer
is sort of obvious.

01:00:14.200 --> 01:00:17.450
That is, the answer
is that those

01:00:17.450 --> 01:00:20.510
of us in the United States, and
in the world, who don't accept

01:00:20.510 --> 01:00:24.530
this idea must
declare very forcibly,

01:00:24.530 --> 01:00:32.600
and act very vigorously
against this idea,

01:00:32.600 --> 01:00:39.920
and insist that the ethical
norms that most decent people

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:49.760
can agree on should be observed,
and that no country should be

01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:57.740
an exception to the rule of
morality in world affairs,

01:00:57.740 --> 01:01:04.180
and that the
children of the world

01:01:04.180 --> 01:01:06.910
should all be seen
as part of a family,

01:01:06.910 --> 01:01:10.810
and that the children of Iraq,
or the children of China,

01:01:10.810 --> 01:01:15.550
or the children of
anywhere in the world

01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:21.520
have the same right to
life as American children.

01:01:21.520 --> 01:01:25.420
Those a very fundamental
moral principles

01:01:25.420 --> 01:01:28.360
which, if our government
doesn't uphold them,

01:01:28.360 --> 01:01:29.920
we must uphold them.

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:32.740
Fortunately, there are
people all over the world

01:01:32.740 --> 01:01:34.630
who want to uphold
those principles,

01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:37.990
and who oppose it when the
United States does not.

01:01:37.990 --> 01:01:42.520
And we saw on
February 15, 2003--

01:01:42.520 --> 01:01:44.980
on the eve of the
invasion of Iraq,

01:01:44.980 --> 01:01:50.410
we saw that amazing moment
when, on one day, 10 to 15

01:01:50.410 --> 01:01:53.590
million people around
the world, in 60,

01:01:53.590 --> 01:01:57.205
70 countries around the world,
demonstrated against that war.

01:02:02.620 --> 01:02:04.240
So there are people
all over the world

01:02:04.240 --> 01:02:09.430
who do not accept the idea
of American exceptionalism.

01:02:09.430 --> 01:02:13.240
When last week, the State
Department issued its report

01:02:13.240 --> 01:02:14.890
on human rights abuses--

01:02:14.890 --> 01:02:17.170
some of you may have seen
it in the newspapers--

01:02:17.170 --> 01:02:20.340
State Department
issued this report on--

01:02:20.340 --> 01:02:21.790
it does this every year.

01:02:21.790 --> 01:02:25.300
It lists countries which are
guilty of torture and other

01:02:25.300 --> 01:02:27.540
abuses of human rights.

01:02:27.540 --> 01:02:31.730
And so it lists a
bunch of countries.

01:02:31.730 --> 01:02:33.647
A number of those
countries are countries

01:02:33.647 --> 01:02:35.230
which are allies of
the United States.

01:02:35.230 --> 01:02:37.355
A number of those are
countries to which the United

01:02:37.355 --> 01:02:40.270
States has sent prisoners.

01:02:40.270 --> 01:02:43.300
And I think, by now, you
know that this notion

01:02:43.300 --> 01:02:46.858
of extraordinary
rendition, where we're not

01:02:46.858 --> 01:02:48.400
going to torture
these people-- we'll

01:02:48.400 --> 01:02:51.730
send them to countries
which will torture them.

01:02:51.730 --> 01:02:53.230
And so the State
Department issues

01:02:53.230 --> 01:02:57.820
this list of countries which
have violated human rights.

01:02:57.820 --> 01:03:00.970
And then when the
report came out,

01:03:00.970 --> 01:03:05.830
there were responses
from around the world

01:03:05.830 --> 01:03:08.230
which said, hey, there's
one country which

01:03:08.230 --> 01:03:11.980
is missing from this list.

01:03:11.980 --> 01:03:13.615
Yes, what about
the United States?

01:03:21.670 --> 01:03:23.710
A Turkish newspaper
said, there's

01:03:23.710 --> 01:03:28.300
not even a mention of the
incidents in Abu Ghraib prison.

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:30.630
No mention of Guantanamo.

01:03:30.630 --> 01:03:35.200
A newspaper in Sydney,
Australia pointed out

01:03:35.200 --> 01:03:38.110
that the United
States Senate sends

01:03:38.110 --> 01:03:39.917
suspects-- remember
suspects are not

01:03:39.917 --> 01:03:42.250
people who have been tried
and found guilty of anything,

01:03:42.250 --> 01:03:46.270
just people who are suspected
of doing something-- sends

01:03:46.270 --> 01:03:51.850
suspects to prisons in Morocco,
Egypt, Libya, Uzbekistan,

01:03:51.850 --> 01:03:56.560
countries that the State
Department says uses torture.

01:03:56.560 --> 01:04:05.270
And so people around
the world, yes, and here

01:04:05.270 --> 01:04:07.160
in the United States.

01:04:07.160 --> 01:04:13.490
And this is something that we
very often are deprived of.

01:04:13.490 --> 01:04:15.200
We're not only
deprived of history.

01:04:15.200 --> 01:04:17.630
We're very often deprived
of immediate history,

01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:21.020
because we're deprived of things
that happen in this country

01:04:21.020 --> 01:04:28.960
that people are doing
that we don't know about,

01:04:28.960 --> 01:04:31.140
because this is a big country.

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:33.622
But that would be
an easy excuse,

01:04:33.622 --> 01:04:35.080
that we're a big
country and that's

01:04:35.080 --> 01:04:36.288
why we don't know about them.

01:04:36.288 --> 01:04:36.788
No.

01:04:36.788 --> 01:04:38.500
We don't know about
these things that are

01:04:38.500 --> 01:04:40.540
happening around the country--

01:04:40.540 --> 01:04:44.380
these protests, these
declarations of humanity,

01:04:44.380 --> 01:04:48.880
we don't know about them because
they're not really reported.

01:04:48.880 --> 01:04:54.820
And so if you go to the
internet, you might find out.

01:04:54.820 --> 01:04:58.570
If you go to a rally, you might
get some of this information.

01:04:58.570 --> 01:05:01.660
If you travel around the
country, you might learn that--

01:05:04.033 --> 01:05:05.533
this coming weekend,
there are going

01:05:05.533 --> 01:05:08.105
to be demonstrations in
cities all over the country.

01:05:08.105 --> 01:05:10.230
There's going to be a
demonstration in New Orleans.

01:05:10.230 --> 01:05:15.410
There's going to be a
demonstration in Fayetteville,

01:05:15.410 --> 01:05:18.180
North Carolina.

01:05:18.180 --> 01:05:23.020
And there's a resistance
movement in the United States

01:05:23.020 --> 01:05:24.140
to this war.

01:05:24.140 --> 01:05:29.860
And you see only the
superficial, you might say,

01:05:29.860 --> 01:05:32.920
recognition of this, when
you read public opinion

01:05:32.920 --> 01:05:36.700
polls, which show that now,
about half the country does not

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:37.930
believe in the war.

01:05:37.930 --> 01:05:40.240
And if half the country
does not believe in the war,

01:05:40.240 --> 01:05:43.300
then somewhere among
that 50%, there

01:05:43.300 --> 01:05:47.110
must be many, many people who
are actively opposing that war.

01:05:47.110 --> 01:05:52.990
And those people are, in
fact, engaged in protests

01:05:52.990 --> 01:05:54.580
all over the United States.

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:56.860
And what's perhaps
most significant

01:05:56.860 --> 01:05:59.740
is that, in the armed forces
and in the families of the armed

01:05:59.740 --> 01:06:06.330
forces, there is more and
more defection from this war.

01:06:06.330 --> 01:06:10.810
The Iraqi Veterans
Against the War

01:06:10.810 --> 01:06:16.810
formed in kind of reprise of
what we had during Vietnam--

01:06:16.810 --> 01:06:19.860
Vietnam Veterans
Against the War.

01:06:19.860 --> 01:06:23.410
And we have military
families speak out

01:06:23.410 --> 01:06:28.540
of the families of
soldiers organizing,

01:06:28.540 --> 01:06:31.210
and who now have
thousands of members.

01:06:31.210 --> 01:06:33.740
And GIs themselves,
GIs in the field--

01:06:33.740 --> 01:06:39.260
and you've read about instances
of mutiny and GIs who say,

01:06:39.260 --> 01:06:41.020
we don't want to--
who've been to Iraq,

01:06:41.020 --> 01:06:44.290
who are being sent back,
who don't want to go back.

01:06:44.290 --> 01:06:46.360
And some of them go to
Canada, and some of them

01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:47.920
get court-martialed.

01:06:51.950 --> 01:06:53.750
This is important,
because I think

01:06:53.750 --> 01:06:59.660
of what Einstein said when--

01:06:59.660 --> 01:07:04.310
after World War I,
horrified by that war

01:07:04.310 --> 01:07:07.580
and by the idea of war
itself, and by the knowledge

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:12.740
that now, modern warfare would
be indiscriminate and massive.

01:07:15.550 --> 01:07:21.035
And Einstein said, wars will
stop when men refuse to fight.

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:28.970
And so the refusal to fight,
and refusal of families

01:07:28.970 --> 01:07:33.230
to let their kids fight, and
the insistence of the parents

01:07:33.230 --> 01:07:36.770
of high school kids that
they will not let recruiters

01:07:36.770 --> 01:07:39.020
come into the high schools
and approach their kids,

01:07:39.020 --> 01:07:42.270
all of these things--

01:07:42.270 --> 01:07:45.470
these things are
consistent with what

01:07:45.470 --> 01:07:51.490
Einstein thought was ultimately
the way that wars would stop.

01:07:51.490 --> 01:07:58.528
So I leave you with the
idea that we're not alone,

01:07:58.528 --> 01:08:00.820
and that there are people
all over the world and people

01:08:00.820 --> 01:08:03.820
in this country
who do not accept

01:08:03.820 --> 01:08:06.310
the idea of a
special dispensation

01:08:06.310 --> 01:08:07.930
to do whatever we
want in the world,

01:08:07.930 --> 01:08:14.960
and that we'll insist on human
equality of people everywhere.

01:08:14.960 --> 01:08:22.090
And I think of William Lloyd
Garrison, the abolitionist.

01:08:22.090 --> 01:08:25.149
And I think of what
was on the masthead

01:08:25.149 --> 01:08:30.189
of his antislavery
newspaper, The Liberator.

01:08:30.189 --> 01:08:37.569
On his masthead were the
words, my country is the world,

01:08:37.569 --> 01:08:42.569
my country are mankind--

01:08:42.569 --> 01:08:44.602
a good thing to
remember Thank you.

01:08:44.602 --> 01:08:46.574
[APPLAUSE]

01:09:02.510 --> 01:09:04.780
SANYAL: We have around
15 minutes for questions.

01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:08.210
[INAUDIBLE]

01:09:23.890 --> 01:09:27.000
AUDIENCE: Hi, professor.

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:29.700
My question was, do the
people in our government--

01:09:29.700 --> 01:09:32.620
do they believe
their own rhetoric?

01:09:32.620 --> 01:09:35.580
Because I feel it must take an
extreme suspension of belief

01:09:35.580 --> 01:09:39.310
to believe a lot of this
rhetoric of democracy

01:09:39.310 --> 01:09:42.269
and American freedom
being spread everywhere.

01:09:44.819 --> 01:09:46.710
Or do they act knowingly?

01:09:46.710 --> 01:09:49.410
And if they act blindly, is
it because of the structure

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:52.100
of our educational systems
and our government,

01:09:52.100 --> 01:09:56.775
or do they simply act with
the knowledge of disbelief

01:09:56.775 --> 01:09:57.545
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:10:05.960 --> 01:10:12.395
ZINN: [INAUDIBLE] This
will be just between us.

01:10:17.345 --> 01:10:20.300
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
they actually believe what

01:10:20.300 --> 01:10:20.800
[INAUDIBLE]

01:10:20.800 --> 01:10:23.165
ZINN: Oh, oh.

01:10:23.165 --> 01:10:30.410
AUDIENCE: And if they didn't
believe it, [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:30.410 --> 01:10:37.045
educational systems [INAUDIBLE]
government [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:37.045 --> 01:10:39.420
MODERATOR: Please use the
microphones for the video tape.

01:10:44.270 --> 01:10:46.790
ZINN: Did you hear the question?

01:10:46.790 --> 01:10:47.810
Okay.

01:10:47.810 --> 01:10:50.300
Do the people in government,
to do what they do, do

01:10:50.300 --> 01:10:55.490
they believe in what
they do, or are they

01:10:55.490 --> 01:11:00.140
brainwashed by themselves?

01:11:05.360 --> 01:11:09.560
That's a tough question.

01:11:09.560 --> 01:11:11.698
And I don't think I
have an answer to this.

01:11:11.698 --> 01:11:13.490
I don't know if anybody
has an answer this,

01:11:13.490 --> 01:11:14.840
because I think there's--

01:11:14.840 --> 01:11:18.180
I think the answer varies
from person to person.

01:11:18.180 --> 01:11:22.250
I think there are people who do
understand what they're doing

01:11:22.250 --> 01:11:24.500
and don't care.

01:11:24.500 --> 01:11:27.540
They know what they want
and they're going to get it.

01:11:27.540 --> 01:11:29.540
And they don't care, and
they're willing to lie.

01:11:29.540 --> 01:11:32.600
But the lies are
acceptable because it's

01:11:32.600 --> 01:11:35.410
going to achieve the
results that they want.

01:11:35.410 --> 01:11:38.100
These people are cynical people.

01:11:38.100 --> 01:11:40.470
They know what they're doing.

01:11:40.470 --> 01:11:45.510
And there are other people, I
think, who persuade themselves.

01:11:45.510 --> 01:11:53.960
The human mind has an infinite
capacity for self-deception,

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:55.940
and therefore,
there are people--

01:11:55.940 --> 01:11:59.270
I'm sure there are people in
government who really believe

01:11:59.270 --> 01:12:02.150
in what they're doing, who
actually believe that they're

01:12:02.150 --> 01:12:04.220
advancing the cause of
liberty and democracy

01:12:04.220 --> 01:12:06.050
in the world about
what they're doing.

01:12:06.050 --> 01:12:07.508
But as I say, I
don't think there's

01:12:07.508 --> 01:12:12.990
anyone to answer for this great
conglomeration of decision

01:12:12.990 --> 01:12:13.490
makers.

01:12:20.663 --> 01:12:22.080
AUDIENCE: My name
is [INAUDIBLE]..

01:12:22.080 --> 01:12:23.580
I'm one of the
[INAUDIBLE] fellows.

01:12:23.580 --> 01:12:25.000
I'm from Israel.

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:27.330
And I wanted to ask,
which kind of institutions

01:12:27.330 --> 01:12:30.150
in American society
do you think needs

01:12:30.150 --> 01:12:31.930
to implement these moral norms?

01:12:31.930 --> 01:12:34.260
Because I was looking at
the United States policy

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:35.880
in the last two years--

01:12:35.880 --> 01:12:39.650
the detainees at Guantanamo
and about the judiciary--

01:12:39.650 --> 01:12:42.300
and I was seeing
that the judiciary

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:44.430
was more interested
in gay marriage

01:12:44.430 --> 01:12:46.620
than in detainees' rights.

01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:49.440
And I was asking myself,
which kind of institutes

01:12:49.440 --> 01:12:51.615
will handle these
kind of moral issues

01:12:51.615 --> 01:12:52.740
that you're speaking about?

01:12:52.740 --> 01:12:53.940
ZINN: You say what kind of--

01:12:53.940 --> 01:12:54.540
AUDIENCE: Institution.

01:12:54.540 --> 01:12:55.530
ZINN: Institution.

01:13:02.030 --> 01:13:08.640
Especially in countries
that have institutions

01:13:08.640 --> 01:13:15.450
that seem democratic and
that, on the surface,

01:13:15.450 --> 01:13:17.880
will guarantee our
liberties, I think

01:13:17.880 --> 01:13:21.030
that a country like
the United States,

01:13:21.030 --> 01:13:23.450
we tend to overvalue
institutions.

01:13:23.450 --> 01:13:26.610
And very often, we think
that, if a situation is bad,

01:13:26.610 --> 01:13:29.700
we can correct it by setting
up another institution

01:13:29.700 --> 01:13:32.515
or by amending the Constitution.

01:13:32.515 --> 01:13:34.890
I can't tell you how many
times people have approached me

01:13:34.890 --> 01:13:40.042
and said, I have the following
amendments to the Constitution.

01:13:40.042 --> 01:13:42.250
Don't you think that, if we
adopted these amendments,

01:13:42.250 --> 01:13:44.430
that everything-- no.

01:13:44.430 --> 01:13:49.700
Institutions are all malleable,
subject to interpretation.

01:13:49.700 --> 01:13:51.660
We can see that with
the Constitution.

01:13:51.660 --> 01:13:54.570
We can see that with even
the Supreme Court, which

01:13:54.570 --> 01:13:58.290
claims to be a strict
interpreter of whatever

01:13:58.290 --> 01:14:00.780
the Constitution says.

01:14:00.780 --> 01:14:07.560
And no, all these institutions
depend on who has the power.

01:14:07.560 --> 01:14:11.700
And laws are violated with
impunity by the government.

01:14:11.700 --> 01:14:13.740
It doesn't matter
what laws you pass.

01:14:13.740 --> 01:14:16.820
You can pass a law limiting
the powers of the FBI.

01:14:16.820 --> 01:14:23.460
Won't matter, because the FBI
doesn't have to obey the law,

01:14:23.460 --> 01:14:31.650
because if the FBI violates
the law, who will go after it?

01:14:31.650 --> 01:14:33.970
The FBI?

01:14:33.970 --> 01:14:38.630
We have a long history of
government a violation of law,

01:14:38.630 --> 01:14:47.217
and so the answer doesn't lie
in institutions or even in laws.

01:14:47.217 --> 01:14:49.050
Now, it helps to have
some laws, rather than

01:14:49.050 --> 01:14:52.920
other laws, but the--

01:14:52.920 --> 01:14:55.560
those aren't critical.

01:14:55.560 --> 01:14:58.800
We changed the Constitution
at the end of the Civil War

01:14:58.800 --> 01:15:03.540
to give black people,
well, freedom from slavery,

01:15:03.540 --> 01:15:06.660
presumably equal rights, with
the 14th Amendment, the right

01:15:06.660 --> 01:15:08.490
to vote, with the
15th Amendment.

01:15:08.490 --> 01:15:10.560
And so there, we had
institutionalized

01:15:10.560 --> 01:15:11.670
racial equality.

01:15:11.670 --> 01:15:16.020
Didn't matter, because
the 14th and 15th--

01:15:16.020 --> 01:15:18.120
and even to a certain
extent, the 13th,

01:15:18.120 --> 01:15:21.120
because blacks were really
put back in a semi-slavery

01:15:21.120 --> 01:15:24.420
by their lack of resources--
but the 14th and 15th Amendments

01:15:24.420 --> 01:15:28.260
was simply unenforced.

01:15:28.260 --> 01:15:30.600
Not only were they unenforced,
but the 14th Amendment,

01:15:30.600 --> 01:15:37.230
presumably passed to assure
equality for black people--

01:15:37.230 --> 01:15:40.500
14th Amendment became a
tool for corporations,

01:15:40.500 --> 01:15:44.750
to protect corporations against
governmental regulation.

01:15:44.750 --> 01:15:50.650
And so the laws, institutions
are not critical, as I say,

01:15:50.650 --> 01:15:53.300
sure, it's better to--

01:15:53.300 --> 01:15:55.770
if you can set up
better institutions,

01:15:55.770 --> 01:15:58.640
if you can put
better laws in, fine.

01:15:58.640 --> 01:16:01.560
But that is never enough.

01:16:01.560 --> 01:16:05.580
It takes citizen action.

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:07.890
When we've had changes--

01:16:07.890 --> 01:16:10.740
important social changes
take place in this country,

01:16:10.740 --> 01:16:12.690
it hasn't come as
a result of changes

01:16:12.690 --> 01:16:18.330
in institutions-- certainly
not changes in who is elected.

01:16:18.330 --> 01:16:22.740
No, it's come through the
growth of social movements.

01:16:22.740 --> 01:16:29.130
And that was true of
winning a degree of freedom

01:16:29.130 --> 01:16:35.550
for ex-slaves, and it's true
of winning rights of workers,

01:16:35.550 --> 01:16:38.610
and true in-- well, in
recent years, whatever rights

01:16:38.610 --> 01:16:43.470
have been won by women,
or by disabled people,

01:16:43.470 --> 01:16:45.780
or by black people.

01:16:45.780 --> 01:16:51.540
They have not come
simply through the change

01:16:51.540 --> 01:16:52.890
in institutions.

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:55.920
Although that may accompany
the social movements.

01:16:55.920 --> 01:16:58.200
That come out of the
social movements.

01:16:58.200 --> 01:17:02.370
But basically, it's citizen
action, and organization,

01:17:02.370 --> 01:17:07.850
and willing to take risks on
behalf of important values.

01:17:07.850 --> 01:17:08.850
Those have been crucial.

01:17:13.165 --> 01:17:16.360
AUDIENCE: My name's [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:16.360 --> 01:17:18.520
I wouldn't think it would
be exceptional to think

01:17:18.520 --> 01:17:22.620
that this problem is a uniquely
American problem, and that,

01:17:22.620 --> 01:17:26.080
over the course of history,
that numerous societies assumed

01:17:26.080 --> 01:17:28.300
that they were exceptional--

01:17:28.300 --> 01:17:31.713
the Romans, the
British, the Japanese--

01:17:31.713 --> 01:17:32.880
at different points in time.

01:17:32.880 --> 01:17:34.870
So as a historian,
can you give us

01:17:34.870 --> 01:17:39.832
any examples where societies
have successfully climbed down

01:17:39.832 --> 01:17:45.020
from this progenerating
myth about themselves

01:17:45.020 --> 01:17:49.270
to actually assuming
that they're

01:17:49.270 --> 01:17:51.310
equal with other people?

01:17:51.310 --> 01:17:53.520
Is there any success stories?

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:58.060
ZINN: Historical
examples of where

01:17:58.060 --> 01:18:03.370
nations have divested themselves
of this exceptionalism,

01:18:03.370 --> 01:18:05.740
and accepted the idea of
equality with other people?

01:18:09.160 --> 01:18:15.320
Only when they were forced to.

01:18:15.320 --> 01:18:18.600
That is, the British
Empire was dismantled,

01:18:18.600 --> 01:18:25.190
but not out of sheer generosity.

01:18:25.190 --> 01:18:29.200
But it was forced to dismantle.

01:18:29.200 --> 01:18:34.220
And no, I don't know
of any government which

01:18:34.220 --> 01:18:39.870
has claimed exceptional
position for itself,

01:18:39.870 --> 01:18:46.320
which has given up that in
deference to human rights.

01:18:46.320 --> 01:18:49.880
But governments and
empires in the past

01:18:49.880 --> 01:18:57.320
have fallen and have
been forced to retrench,

01:18:57.320 --> 01:19:04.250
as the Belgians in Africa, and
the British and French in Asia,

01:19:04.250 --> 01:19:08.700
but only when compelled
to by resistance.

01:19:17.030 --> 01:19:19.100
AUDIENCE: When speaking
about exceptionalism,

01:19:19.100 --> 01:19:22.460
you were talking
about a outside threat

01:19:22.460 --> 01:19:28.340
helping to justify a
lot of US aggression

01:19:28.340 --> 01:19:33.480
and taking on imperial actions.

01:19:33.480 --> 01:19:37.980
I was wondering, prior to the
justification of communism,

01:19:37.980 --> 01:19:41.460
what was the US
justification for expansion?

01:19:41.460 --> 01:19:45.150
And what was it--

01:19:45.150 --> 01:19:48.270
as part of a reflection of the
time that we all live through,

01:19:48.270 --> 01:19:52.800
what was the prevailing thought
between the fall of communism

01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:58.420
and the prevailing
idea of terrorism now?

01:19:58.420 --> 01:20:02.460
ZINN: Well, so two
questions, I guess.

01:20:08.830 --> 01:20:12.400
What was our justification
before communism?

01:20:12.400 --> 01:20:18.370
[INAUDIBLE] different
justifications

01:20:18.370 --> 01:20:23.020
over those several centuries.

01:20:28.030 --> 01:20:32.800
In the case of, for instance,
our wars against the Indians

01:20:32.800 --> 01:20:35.260
and our expansion
there, very often,

01:20:35.260 --> 01:20:42.730
the justification was that
we were civilizing them.

01:20:42.730 --> 01:20:47.780
They were savage
group, and we were--

01:20:47.780 --> 01:20:49.710
and therefore, we
were civilizing them.

01:20:49.710 --> 01:20:53.230
And when we conquered them, we
tried to take the young people

01:20:53.230 --> 01:20:54.820
and put them into
special schools

01:20:54.820 --> 01:20:57.290
to teach them
civilization, and so on.

01:20:57.290 --> 01:21:02.910
So as civilizing other
people who were backward,

01:21:02.910 --> 01:21:05.497
there was a certain
amount of justification

01:21:05.497 --> 01:21:07.080
for that in the
Mexican War certainly,

01:21:07.080 --> 01:21:09.160
in the Philippine War.

01:21:09.160 --> 01:21:11.740
And as I pointed out, in
Cuba, the justification

01:21:11.740 --> 01:21:14.230
was liberating the Cubans.

01:21:14.230 --> 01:21:17.840
In the case of our forays
into Central America--

01:21:17.840 --> 01:21:23.200
of which there were many, many
marine forays into Central

01:21:23.200 --> 01:21:28.060
America in the early
part of the 20th century,

01:21:28.060 --> 01:21:31.300
culminating in, you might say,
the long occupation of Haiti

01:21:31.300 --> 01:21:34.210
and a long occupation
of Dominican Republic--

01:21:34.210 --> 01:21:38.720
the justification was
that there was disorder

01:21:38.720 --> 01:21:46.185
and we were bringing
order to these countries.

01:21:46.185 --> 01:21:47.560
And in the case
of, for instance,

01:21:47.560 --> 01:21:49.480
of Theodore Roosevelt,
who developed

01:21:49.480 --> 01:21:51.310
something called the
Roosevelt corollary,

01:21:51.310 --> 01:21:53.050
the justification was--

01:21:53.050 --> 01:21:56.050
corollary to the
Monroe Doctrine--

01:21:56.050 --> 01:22:00.230
justification was, well, he's--

01:22:00.230 --> 01:22:01.760
they're unstable.

01:22:01.760 --> 01:22:06.700
They're not able to take care
of their own finances, which

01:22:06.700 --> 01:22:09.400
means they really can't take
care of their obligations

01:22:09.400 --> 01:22:10.750
to the United States.

01:22:10.750 --> 01:22:12.650
We don't like that.

01:22:12.650 --> 01:22:15.340
Sort of their varying
sparing reasons

01:22:15.340 --> 01:22:22.240
given for all of these
foreign policy actions

01:22:22.240 --> 01:22:33.190
before communism became a
easy, monolithic excuse.

01:22:33.190 --> 01:22:36.580
And you're asking about
what about between the fall

01:22:36.580 --> 01:22:44.360
of the Soviet Union and
the war on terrorism.

01:22:44.360 --> 01:22:55.066
Well, I guess you see an
example in the war on Panama,

01:22:55.066 --> 01:22:59.433
or the first Gulf War.

01:22:59.433 --> 01:23:00.850
The war on Panama
takes place just

01:23:00.850 --> 01:23:03.760
at the point with the Soviet
Union is falling apart.

01:23:03.760 --> 01:23:05.440
The justification
of the war in Panama

01:23:05.440 --> 01:23:14.620
is, well, Noriega bad man.

01:23:19.450 --> 01:23:20.680
He no longer likes us.

01:23:20.680 --> 01:23:21.790
He did at one time.

01:23:21.790 --> 01:23:23.230
He worked for us.

01:23:23.230 --> 01:23:25.810
Doesn't want to
work for us anymore.

01:23:25.810 --> 01:23:28.005
And furthermore, he's
involved in the drug trade.

01:23:28.005 --> 01:23:30.130
And we want to get him,
and we want to do something

01:23:30.130 --> 01:23:31.540
about the drug trade.

01:23:31.540 --> 01:23:35.770
Well, if you know what's
happening with the drug trade

01:23:35.770 --> 01:23:39.430
today, you'd be
dubious about that.

01:23:42.400 --> 01:23:44.366
By the way, there's a--

01:23:44.366 --> 01:23:48.040
if you want to read something
more interesting than the kinds

01:23:48.040 --> 01:23:52.300
of things I tell you about
history and about Panama,

01:23:52.300 --> 01:23:57.250
read Johns le Carré's
novel, The Taylor of Panama.

01:23:57.250 --> 01:24:00.660
In John le Carré's novel
The Tailor of Panama,

01:24:00.660 --> 01:24:03.760
he has a small section in which
he deals with the American

01:24:03.760 --> 01:24:09.370
invasion of Panama and
the mayhem that we created

01:24:09.370 --> 01:24:10.360
in Panama.

01:24:10.360 --> 01:24:12.700
But then, of course,
the first Gulf War

01:24:12.700 --> 01:24:19.330
took place between the collapse
of the Soviet Union and the war

01:24:19.330 --> 01:24:20.030
on terrorism.

01:24:20.030 --> 01:24:22.600
What was the reason there?

01:24:22.600 --> 01:24:24.150
Well, the reason there--

01:24:24.150 --> 01:24:26.710
the justification
given for that was

01:24:26.710 --> 01:24:35.530
that Iraq had invaded Kuwait,
which is certainly true,

01:24:35.530 --> 01:24:39.110
and certainly not justifiable.

01:24:39.110 --> 01:24:42.400
But there's an interesting
leap that takes place

01:24:42.400 --> 01:24:47.830
in thinking about war.

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:51.250
It's a leap from a just
cause to a just war.

01:24:54.025 --> 01:24:58.750
A cause may be just,
and that is that--

01:24:58.750 --> 01:25:00.850
keeping Kuwait out of
Saddam Hussein's hands

01:25:00.850 --> 01:25:03.190
is a just cause.

01:25:03.190 --> 01:25:07.570
But then you're making a
jump, if you immediately

01:25:07.570 --> 01:25:11.530
rush from that to the idea of,
therefore, war is required.

01:25:11.530 --> 01:25:14.500
Just as in Korea, we--

01:25:14.500 --> 01:25:19.090
it was unjust for North Korea
to move into South Korea, but--

01:25:19.090 --> 01:25:22.210
so there was a just
cause there, but that

01:25:22.210 --> 01:25:24.460
leaves open the question
of whether then,

01:25:24.460 --> 01:25:30.610
the proper response, the human
response the best response

01:25:30.610 --> 01:25:35.740
to that was to go to war, and
therefore, would be a just war.

01:25:35.740 --> 01:25:38.290
Well, not quite.

01:25:38.290 --> 01:25:42.620
We know now [INAUDIBLE] have
three years of war in Korea.

01:25:42.620 --> 01:25:47.110
Two or three million people dead
in Korea, and at the end of it,

01:25:47.110 --> 01:25:50.710
we were back where we were
when the war started back, back

01:25:50.710 --> 01:25:54.122
to North Korea dictatorship,
South Korea dictatorship.

01:25:57.230 --> 01:25:58.750
So it was a just cause.

01:25:58.750 --> 01:26:00.610
The question is, should
we have gone to war?

01:26:04.030 --> 01:26:05.410
Motivation is important.

01:26:08.710 --> 01:26:15.970
It is important to ask whether
motivations that are expressed

01:26:15.970 --> 01:26:17.800
are honest ones.

01:26:17.800 --> 01:26:21.860
And I think it's worth
thinking about this question--

01:26:21.860 --> 01:26:28.220
did George Bush Senior go
to war against Iraq in 1991

01:26:28.220 --> 01:26:32.490
because he was heartbroken
over the invasion of Kuwait?

01:26:34.902 --> 01:26:35.860
That's hard to believe.

01:26:39.590 --> 01:26:41.230
There've been other invasions.

01:26:41.230 --> 01:26:43.540
There've been other occupations.

01:26:43.540 --> 01:26:46.030
We don't go to war over every
invasion, every occupation.

01:26:46.030 --> 01:26:48.820
Is it possible that there's
something about Iraq

01:26:48.820 --> 01:26:51.220
and its oil reserves
and the Middle East

01:26:51.220 --> 01:26:56.470
that prompted, again, the
real taking over of Kuwait,

01:26:56.470 --> 01:27:03.400
but made that a very convenient
excuse to move into Iraq

01:27:03.400 --> 01:27:06.390
and then to establish
bases there?

01:27:06.390 --> 01:27:11.050
And the bases that we
established in Saudi Arabia

01:27:11.050 --> 01:27:13.330
came directly out of that war.

01:27:18.240 --> 01:27:21.395
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE]
Last question.

01:27:21.395 --> 01:27:23.140
ZINN: Last question.

01:27:23.140 --> 01:27:23.640
Oh.

01:27:26.210 --> 01:27:28.560
AUDIENCE: My name's James.

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:31.580
There's a current of
American exceptionalism,

01:27:31.580 --> 01:27:34.010
if I'm not mistaken,
that has to do

01:27:34.010 --> 01:27:37.340
with trying to explain
why there hasn't been

01:27:37.340 --> 01:27:41.030
a strong social
democracy in America

01:27:41.030 --> 01:27:46.990
like there has been in some, if
not many, European countries.

01:27:46.990 --> 01:27:49.400
And I think probably the most--

01:27:49.400 --> 01:27:53.300
the best-known exponent
of this is probably if--

01:27:53.300 --> 01:27:55.070
and correct me,
if I'm mistaken--

01:27:55.070 --> 01:27:58.010
Seymour Martin Lipset, the
Canadian political scientist,

01:27:58.010 --> 01:28:01.460
I think, is one of the
best-known to put forward

01:28:01.460 --> 01:28:05.120
this attempt to explain
American exceptionalism,

01:28:05.120 --> 01:28:11.550
ie the absence of a strong
social democracy, or even

01:28:11.550 --> 01:28:13.130
a stronger socialist movement.

01:28:16.360 --> 01:28:22.640
What's your take on this current
of what is sometimes referred

01:28:22.640 --> 01:28:24.440
to as American exceptionalism?

01:28:24.440 --> 01:28:27.170
But more importantly,
what's your take

01:28:27.170 --> 01:28:33.160
on the whole idea of trying
to come to terms with quote,

01:28:33.160 --> 01:28:37.540
unquote, "why there
hasn't been a stronger

01:28:37.540 --> 01:28:41.120
movement for social democracy--

01:28:41.120 --> 01:28:44.930
realized social democracy
in the United States?

01:28:44.930 --> 01:28:46.780
And parenthetically,
how much would you

01:28:46.780 --> 01:28:50.590
say the incredible
diversity in this country

01:28:50.590 --> 01:28:53.620
is part of the
challenge that anybody

01:28:53.620 --> 01:28:56.680
interested in that
programmatically

01:28:56.680 --> 01:28:59.410
has to come to terms with?

01:28:59.410 --> 01:29:07.130
ZINN: Well, James, you
know that's a big question.

01:29:07.130 --> 01:29:09.320
And I don't deal
with big questions.

01:29:09.320 --> 01:29:10.970
[INAUDIBLE] little ones.

01:29:10.970 --> 01:29:12.340
I like little questions.

01:29:19.020 --> 01:29:22.902
Well, you mentioned diversity.

01:29:22.902 --> 01:29:25.200
This is an issue which
American historians

01:29:25.200 --> 01:29:27.030
and political
scientists [INAUDIBLE]

01:29:27.030 --> 01:29:29.410
have grappled with
for a very long time.

01:29:29.410 --> 01:29:31.860
So here I am MIT on
this day, and I'm going

01:29:31.860 --> 01:29:34.710
to give the definitive, final--

01:29:34.710 --> 01:29:36.780
after all these people
have worked on this,

01:29:36.780 --> 01:29:39.480
I am going to tell you
what the real truth is.

01:29:39.480 --> 01:29:40.305
Yes, of course.

01:29:46.080 --> 01:29:47.580
You can give a
number of reasons why

01:29:47.580 --> 01:29:50.850
we haven't had a very powerful,
radical, left movement

01:29:50.850 --> 01:29:52.370
in the United States.

01:29:52.370 --> 01:29:54.240
And you can find,
yes, diversity--

01:29:54.240 --> 01:29:59.460
the size of the country, all
the conflicts between immigrants

01:29:59.460 --> 01:30:03.120
and native-born, they use the
pitting of people against one

01:30:03.120 --> 01:30:06.870
another-- black against white--

01:30:06.870 --> 01:30:09.450
the reformism of
the United States,

01:30:09.450 --> 01:30:13.590
the doling out of little
goodies to the middle class

01:30:13.590 --> 01:30:15.510
to create a large
enough middle class

01:30:15.510 --> 01:30:19.150
to act as a buffer between the
very rich and the very poor.

01:30:19.150 --> 01:30:24.630
There are a number of
reasons, I think, for the fact

01:30:24.630 --> 01:30:27.760
that there hasn't been a
powerful movement in the United

01:30:27.760 --> 01:30:28.260
States.

01:30:28.260 --> 01:30:30.790
But I would add one more thing.

01:30:30.790 --> 01:30:35.730
I think there's a tendency on
the part of a lot of people--

01:30:35.730 --> 01:30:38.010
I would never accuse
you of this, James--

01:30:38.010 --> 01:30:43.090
but there are
people who, I think,

01:30:43.090 --> 01:30:46.360
make too great a distinction
between the United States--

01:30:46.360 --> 01:30:49.658
[INAUDIBLE] who see too
much exceptionalism there--

01:30:49.658 --> 01:30:51.700
too great a distinction
between the United States

01:30:51.700 --> 01:30:59.250
and other post-industrial
countries.

01:30:59.250 --> 01:31:02.550
As if, well, these countries
have great social movements--

01:31:02.550 --> 01:31:08.820
England, France, Italy-- but
the United States lags behind,

01:31:08.820 --> 01:31:09.780
I don't--

01:31:09.780 --> 01:31:13.740
I reject the idea of
that sharp a distinction

01:31:13.740 --> 01:31:16.950
between these
countries in Europe.

01:31:16.950 --> 01:31:19.710
Granted that, sure, there's
a big powerful trade union

01:31:19.710 --> 01:31:22.290
movement in France, and there
isn't a powerful trade union

01:31:22.290 --> 01:31:23.310
movement here.

01:31:23.310 --> 01:31:28.230
But we had important
social movements

01:31:28.230 --> 01:31:32.100
in this country
from time to time,

01:31:32.100 --> 01:31:34.300
and we'll probably
have one again,

01:31:34.300 --> 01:31:38.130
especially if things
continue the way they are.

01:31:38.130 --> 01:31:43.420
And I think it's too
easy to denigrate

01:31:43.420 --> 01:31:47.070
the amount of social protest,
and social organization,

01:31:47.070 --> 01:31:51.827
and resistance that there
has been in this country

01:31:51.827 --> 01:31:52.785
throughout our history.

01:31:55.380 --> 01:31:56.950
At the beginning of
the 20th century,

01:31:56.950 --> 01:32:00.130
we had a very powerful socialist
movement in this country.

01:32:00.130 --> 01:32:02.190
We had a radical labor
unions at the beginning

01:32:02.190 --> 01:32:04.548
of the 20th century, and
then again in the '30s,

01:32:04.548 --> 01:32:05.090
with the CIO.

01:32:11.460 --> 01:32:14.940
The great movements of the
'60s are very important social

01:32:14.940 --> 01:32:18.150
movements You might say more--

01:32:18.150 --> 01:32:24.820
certainly more-- which had no
counterpart really in Europe,

01:32:24.820 --> 01:32:28.660
except France '68, and so on.

01:32:28.660 --> 01:32:30.900
But I guess what I'm
saying is I don't think

01:32:30.900 --> 01:32:35.190
we're as exceptional
as a lot of people

01:32:35.190 --> 01:32:38.910
make us out to be
in lagging behind

01:32:38.910 --> 01:32:41.790
in the development
of social movements

01:32:41.790 --> 01:32:43.538
and social consciousness.

01:32:43.538 --> 01:32:44.580
We have a long way to go.

01:32:47.258 --> 01:32:48.300
That's the last question?

01:32:48.300 --> 01:32:49.500
[APPLAUSE]

01:32:50.100 --> 01:32:51.650
Okay.

